WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.624
Train, dev, test, and what the gap tells you.

2
00:00:02.904 --> 00:00:07.405
At 53:20 the lecture splits the names 80/10/10.

3
00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:09.179
The split is a contract.

4
00:00:09.459 --> 00:00:11.293
The training set fits parameters.

5
00:00:11.573 --> 00:00:17.888
The dev (validation) set chooses hyperparameters: hidden size, embedding size, learning rate, steps.

6
00:00:18.168 --> 00:00:23.608
The test set is evaluated once, at the end, to report a number no decision depended on.

7
00:00:23.888 --> 00:00:30.309
Use the test set to choose anything and it becomes a second dev set, and your reported score is optimistic.

8
00:00:30.589 --> 00:00:33.107
The two curves you track tell you what to do next.

9
00:00:33.387 --> 00:00:48.789
If training and dev loss are close and both high, the model is underfitting: it lacks capacity, so make it bigger (the video grows the hidden layer at 01:00:49, then the embeddings at 01:07:16).

10
00:00:49.069 --> 00:00:58.883
If training loss keeps falling while dev loss stalls or rises, it is overfitting: it is memorizing, so get more data, regularize, or stop earlier.

11
00:00:59.163 --> 00:01:04.517
The video’s exercise E01 asks you to beat its best validation loss of 2.

12
00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:08.637
2 by tuning; when you do, you’ll be making exactly these calls.

13
00:01:08.917 --> 00:01:10.837
If this feels familiar, it should.

14
00:01:11.117 --> 00:01:22.467
Studio 3’s evaluation discipline (a held-out set, decisions on dev, a final untouched test) is the same contract, applied to prompts and models instead of weights.
